From c6114b0e8e20056a58491f2f42ec5f986d7eacea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 18:18:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] Multiview: views of NVIDIA's formats put
 side by side, and each eye moved by a disparity adjustment

The picture of the two views of MVC and MV-HEVC (CMultiviewPacker) was
made of views in the formats of the processor's decoder. Two things are
added to it, for what follows:

- Views in the formats that NVIDIA's decoder gives when they are copied
  from the card: NV12 (8 bits), which Kodi's renderers on GBM take and
  which goes side by side as it is, and P010 (10 bits, each sample in the
  high bits of 16, U and V interleaved), which they do not take: its views
  go into a picture of planar 10 bits (yuv420p10, what the processor's
  decoder gives) in the same pass, sample for sample (PackedFormat(),
  CopyP010(); the low 6 bits of P010 are zero). The loops have no test
  inside, so that the compiler makes vector code of them.
- A disparity adjustment (SetDisparityAdjustment(), KODI::MULTIVIEW::
  LeftEyeShift()): Apple's spatial video carries one ('dadj' in 'cmfy' of
  'eyes'), in ten-thousandths of the width of a view. Apple's
  "QuickTime and ISO Base Media File Formats and Spatial and Immersive
  Media" (1.9.8) applies half of it to each eye, positive denoting more
  disparity from the parallel direction; its Compressor: "When horizontal
  disparity is negative, content in the footage appears closer; when it's
  positive, content seems farther away". So a positive value moves the
  left eye's picture to the left and the right eye's to the right, by whole
  chroma samples, as each is put in its half; the edge it leaves is black
  (limited or full range) and without colour. 0, the default, moves
  nothing.

And for the NVIDIA decoder of both views (next commit), what decides it
(KODI::MULTIVIEW::NvdecForTwoViews()): HEVC only, never against the
player or the setting of hardware decoding, not after it failed for the
stream, and only with a driver of the 610 series or later loaded, the first
that NVIDIA's Video Codec SDK 13.1 asks for ("Driver version 610 and
above"): an older one does not know the fields that say the layer of each
picture of MV-HEVC.

The log says once what the eyes of a stream are and what is made of them.

Checked on the host (tools/regress/mvhevc): the conformance streams of
ITU-T H.265.1 given to the packer as NV12 give each view as the reference
decoder; made-up 10-bit views of 1080p and 2160p given as P010 come out as
the yuv420p10 they were made from; the adjustment, both signs and none,
limited and full range, in yuv420p10, P010 and NV12, sample by sample; six
mutants of these rules caught.
